## Why This Is Asked
Low-level timer logic is common in network and game loops; beginner-friendly but tests memory layout, wrap-around, and basic lock-free thinking without heavy concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Ring buffers and wrap-around
- Pre-allocated object pools
- Scheduling by slot arithmetic and locality
- Minimal locking assumptions in single-threaded loop

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Task { void (*cb)(void*); void* ctx; struct Task* next; } Task;
#define WHEEL_SIZE 256
static Task* wheel[WHEEL_SIZE];
static int cur = 0;
static Task pool[512]; static int pool_free = 512;
void init_wheel(){ for(int i=0;i<WHEEL_SIZE;i++) wheel[i]=NULL; cur=0; pool_free=512; }
static Task* alloc_task(){ return &pool[--pool_free]; }
void schedule(void (*cb)(void*), void* ctx, uint32_t delay_ms){ int slot = (cur + delay_ms) & (WHEEL_SIZE-1); Task* t = alloc_task(); t->cb=cb; t->ctx=ctx; t->next=wheel[slot]; wheel[slot]=t; }
void tick_one_ms(){ Task* t = wheel[cur]; wheel[cur]=NULL; while(t){ Task* next=t->next; t->cb(t->ctx); t = next; } cur=(cur+1)&(WHEEL_SIZE-1); }
```

