## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level synchronization and memory-order reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Ticket-based spinlock yields FIFO fairness
- Atomics: fetch_add, load, store
- Memory orders: acquire on lock, release on unlock, relaxed during waiting

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t next;
  _Atomic uint32_t serve;
} TicketLock;
void init(TicketLock* t){ atomic_store(&t->next,0); atomic_store(&t->serve,0); }
uint32_t lock(TicketLock* t){ uint32_t my = atomic_fetch_add(&t->next,1); while (atomic_load(&t->serve) != my) { _mm_pause(); } return my; }
void unlock(TicketLock* t, uint32_t my){ atomic_store_explicit(&t->serve, my+1, memory_order_release); }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for NUMA or many threads?
- What are practical drawbacks of busy-waiting and how would you verify FIFO fairness under stress?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of low-level memory management, per-thread caching strategies, lock-free data structures, memory ordering semantics, and NUMA awareness in practical allocator design.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size block allocators
- Thread-local storage (TLS) freelists with global slab pool
- Lock-free freelist with ABA avoidance using atomic compare-exchange
- Memory ordering: acquire/release semantics
- NUMA locality considerations and fragmentation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: alloc/free for fixed-size blocks
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
Block* tls_freelist_head;
Block* global_pool_pull();

Block* alloc() {
  Block* b = tls_freelist_head;
  if (!b) {
    b = global_pool_pull();
    if (!b) return NULL; // OOM
  }
  tls_freelist_head = b->next;
  return b;
}

void free(Block* b) {
  b->next = tls_freelist_head;
  tls_freelist_head = b;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

